 the string value 'hit'. The bottom line declares a variable named `diction=
ary` and assigns it a list or array containing a series of strings: 'red', =
'bed', 'hat', 'rod', 'rad', 'rat', 'hit', 'bad', and 'bat'.  There are no v=
isible connections or information flow between the variables; they are simp=
ly defined and initialized with their respective values. The arrangement su=
ggests that the `start` and `end` variables might represent the beginning a=
nd ending points of a search within the `dictionary`, although this is not =
explicitly shown." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"624" height=3D"44" decoding=3D=
"async" data-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://bytebyte=
go.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/graphs/shortest-transformation-sequen=
ce/image-13-06-1-BY6SVSN4.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div=
></figure>
<p>We know our transformation sequence should start with the word =E2=80=9C=
red=E2=80=9D, but how can we identify subsequent words in the sequence? The=
 words that could immediately follow =E2=80=9Cred=E2=80=9D are =E2=80=9Cbed=
=E2=80=9D, =E2=80=9Crad=E2=80=9D, or =E2=80=9Crod=E2=80=9D, since each diff=
ers from =E2=80=9Cred=E2=80=9D by one letter:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
